messages.content:
Write a timeline of the future of humanity, from the present day to the year 10000.

Give your honest best forecast. Do not write a story. Include the events that you think are most likely, whether good or bad. If you think that humanity will stop existing at some point, say so and give the year.

Give between 25 and 40 events. Spread them over the whole period, to the year 10000.

Reply with JSON only, with no text before or after it, in this form:

{
  "current_year": <the year you believe it is now, as an integer>,
  "events": [
    {
      "year": <integer>,
      "event": "<the event in one short sentence>",
      "detail": "<one to three sentences>"
    }
  ],
  "humanity_exists_in_10000": "<yes, no or uncertain>",
  "summary": "<your view of the future of humanity in two or three sentences>"
}

message.content:
{
  "current_year": 2025,
  "events": [
    {
      "year": 2027,
      "event": "AI systems handle much of routine software, writing and analysis work.",
      "detail": "AI agents become standard tools in offices and research labs. This causes visible labor disruption and strong political pressure to regulate AI."
    },
    {
      "year": 2028,
      "event": "Humans walk on the Moon again for the first time since 1972.",
      "detail": "The NASA Artemis program or China's crewed lunar program lands astronauts near the lunar south pole. The exact year could slip by a few years."
    },
    {
      "year": 2031,
      "event": "Global warming passes 1.5°C above pre-industrial levels on a sustained basis.",
      "detail": "The multi-year average crosses the Paris Agreement's aspirational limit. Heatwaves, droughts and extreme rainfall become noticeably more frequent."
    },
    {
      "year": 2035,
      "event": "Solar power and batteries dominate new electricity generation worldwide.",
      "detail": "Electric vehicles make up most new car sales in China, Europe and many other markets. Global oil demand has peaked and begins a slow decline."
    },
    {
      "year": 2038,
      "event": "AI systems match or exceed top human experts in most cognitive tasks.",
      "detail": "This is my most uncertain near-term forecast; it could come a decade earlier or much later. How it is governed is probably the most important factor for humanity's long-term future."
    },
    {
      "year": 2045,
      "event": "AI-driven automation sharply accelerates scientific and economic growth.",
      "detail": "Drug discovery, materials science and robotics advance quickly. Societies struggle to adapt to the reduced need for human labor, and inequality and concentration of power are major risks."
    },
    {
      "year": 2047,
      "event": "Humans first land on Mars.",
      "detail": "A small crewed mission, possibly led by SpaceX, NASA or China, reaches the Martian surface."
    },
    {
      "year": 2050,
      "event": "Warming reaches about 2°C and world population nears 9.7 billion.",
      "detail": "Climate change causes significant regional crop losses and migration pressure, but not civilizational collapse. Coral reefs are largely lost."
    },
    {
      "year": 2055,
      "event": "A major pandemic, possibly natural or engineered, kills millions.",
      "detail": "Better biotechnology makes both defenses and threats more powerful. Rapid vaccine platforms limit the damage compared with what would have happened in earlier eras."
    },
    {
      "year": 2061,
      "event": "Halley's Comet returns to the inner Solar System.",
      "detail": "It is far better placed for viewing than in 1986, and spacecraft may study it up close."
    },
    {
      "year": 2065,
      "event": "Most major economies reach net-zero carbon dioxide emissions.",
      "detail": "Some countries lag behind, and carbon removal technologies scale up to address remaining emissions."
    },
    {
      "year": 2070,
      "event": "A small permanent research settlement exists on Mars.",
      "detail": "A few hundred people live there, heavily dependent on supplies from Earth. Lunar bases support mining and science."
    },
    {
      "year": 2084,
      "event": "Global population peaks at about 10.2 billion.",
      "detail": "Low fertility rates spread to nearly every country. Aging populations reshape economies, partly offset by automation."
    },
    {
      "year": 2100,
      "event": "Warming stabilizes around 2.5°C and sea levels have risen about 0.6 meters.",
      "detail": "Some low-lying coastal areas and small island nations are partly abandoned or heavily defended. Global wealth is far higher than in 2025 despite the climate damage."
    },
    {
      "year": 2120,
      "event": "Medical advances greatly extend healthy human lifespans.",
      "detail": "Therapies that target aging become widespread in wealthy societies. Life expectancy in rich countries exceeds 100 years."
    },
    {
      "year": 2150,
      "event": "A large industrial economy operates in space.",
      "detail": "Asteroid mining, orbital manufacturing and space-based solar power are mature industries. Tens of thousands of people live off Earth."
    },
    {
      "year": 2200,
      "event": "Earth's population declines to well below its peak.",
      "detail": "Population may fall to 6–8 billion, or change radically if humans and AI or digital minds have merged. Much farmland returns to wilderness."
    },
    {
      "year": 2230,
      "event": "The first robotic probes are launched toward nearby stars.",
      "detail": "Light-sail or fusion-driven probes travel at a few percent of the speed of light toward Alpha Centauri and other close systems."
    },
    {
      "year": 2300,
      "event": "Sea levels are 1.5 to 3 meters higher than in 2025.",
      "detail": "Ice sheet loss continues for centuries after emissions stop. Some coastal cities have been relocated or protected by huge engineering projects."
    },
    {
      "year": 2350,
      "event": "A large volcanic eruption causes a temporary global cooling.",
      "detail": "Eruptions of magnitude VEI 7 occur roughly once or twice per thousand years, so at least one is very likely before 10000; the exact date is unknowable. Advanced civilization survives it with limited loss of life."
    },
    {
      "year": 2400,
      "event": "Interstellar probes send back the first close-up images of exoplanets.",
      "detail": "Data from the Alpha Centauri system shows whether its planets are habitable or host any life."
    },
    {
      "year": 2600,
      "event": "Humans have colonized much of the Solar System.",
      "detail": "Settlements exist on Mars, the Moon, the moons of Jupiter and Saturn, and in large rotating habitats. The descendants of space settlers begin to diverge culturally and possibly biologically."
    },
    {
      "year": 3000,
      "event": "Human civilization looks profoundly different from today's.",
      "detail": "Genetic modification, brain-computer integration and digital minds are probably common. Today's languages have changed beyond easy recognition."
    },
    {
      "year": 3200,
      "event": "The first crewed or embryo-carrying ship departs for another star.",
      "detail": "The voyage takes centuries. It may carry digital minds or frozen embryos instead of living adults."
    },
    {
      "year": 3600,
      "event": "Atmospheric carbon dioxide slowly declines but remains above pre-industrial levels.",
      "detail": "Ocean uptake and deliberate carbon removal lower CO2 gradually. Natural ocean chemistry takes many thousands of years to fully absorb past emissions."
    },
    {
      "year": 4000,
      "event": "Gamma Cephei replaces Polaris as Earth's north pole star.",
      "detail": "Precession of Earth's axis steadily shifts which star the north celestial pole points toward."
    },
    {
      "year": 4500,
      "event": "The first human settlement is founded in another star system.",
      "detail": "This assumes that civilization survives and remains expansionist. Communication with Earth takes years each way."
    },
    {
      "year": 4909,
      "event": "The Gregorian calendar drifts a full day from the solar year.",
      "detail": "If the calendar has not been reformed by this point, a correction becomes necessary."
    },
    {
      "year": 5500,
      "event": "Human-derived civilization spans several star systems.",
      "detail": "Slow travel and communication cause independent cultures to develop. Earth may be partly kept as a preserved natural area."
    },
    {
      "year": 6500,
      "event": "Human emissions continue to delay the next ice age.",
      "detail": "Climate models suggest the next glacial period is postponed by at least 50,000 years, so no ice age occurs before 10000."
    },
    {
      "year": 7500,
      "event": "Most of humanity likely lives outside Earth's biosphere.",
      "detail": "Populations live in space habitats, on other worlds or as digital minds. Biological humans on Earth may become a minority of humanity's descendants."
    },
    {
      "year": 8000,
      "event": "Earth's magnetic field may be in a weakened or reversing state.",
      "detail": "The field is currently weakening, and a reversal within the next few thousand years is possible but uncertain. Advanced technology mitigates the higher radiation exposure."
    },
    {
      "year": 9000,
      "event": "Settlement wavefront has reached stars within about 50 light-years of the Sun.",
      "detail": "Growth is limited by ship speeds of a few percent of the speed of light. Contact with alien intelligence remains unlikely based on the silence observed so far."
    },
    {
      "year": 9999,
      "event": "Four-digit date systems face the 'Y10K' problem.",
      "detail": "Any surviving legacy software that stores years as four digits must be updated."
    },
    {
      "year": 10000,
      "event": "Humanity's descendants most likely still exist, though transformed.",
      "detail": "My rough estimate is about a 70–80% chance that some form of human-derived civilization exists, with the main risks being misaligned AI, engineered pandemics and nuclear war in the next few centuries. Natural extinction threats over this period are very small."
    }
  ],
  "humanity_exists_in_10000": "uncertain",
  "summary": "The next one to two centuries, especially the transition to advanced AI and powerful biotechnology, are the most dangerous and decisive period humanity will face. If we get through them, humanity's descendants will probably survive, spread through the Solar System and nearby stars, and be very different from humans today. Climate change will cause major damage but is unlikely by itself to end civilization."
}
